Short:        Free alternative to MUI, OS 3 (test)
Author:       AROS Development Team, Stefan Reinauer, OpenMUI contributors
Type:         util/libs
Version:      [RELEASE] - [STAGE]
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1
Requires:     AmigaOS 3.1+, 68000+, Installer 43+

OpenMUI is a free, open-source alternative to MUI for AmigaOS 3.x.
Install it instead of MUI, and programs made for MUI 3, 4 and 5 work
with it. It comes with everything MUI programs need: muimaster.library,
the classes, and a preferences program. It runs on a stock 68000 with
OS 3.1, and looks and uses memory like MUI 3.8.

OpenMUI is not MUI and is not affiliated with MUI's authors. MUI was
created by Stefan Stuntz; MUI 4 and 5 come from the MUI team. MUI 3.8 was
studied clean-room: its behaviour, never its code.

Where it comes from
-------------------
Zune is the MUI-compatible GUI toolkit of AROS, by The AROS Development
Team. Zune68 is Stefan Reinauer's port of it to classic AmigaOS 3.
OpenMUI is the lean edition of Zune68, with a memory and size diet and
MUI 3.8's look. Earlier test builds of it were called Zune68k lean.

This is a public beta ([STAGE], [RELEASE]). Every figure below was
measured in an emulator (FS-UAE, A1200 with OS 3.1), not on a real Amiga.

What is in it
-------------
- muimaster.library [VERSION], the one library (earlier test builds also
  installed it as zunemaster.library for the preferences program; the
  installer takes that copy away, into its backup if it was not its own).
- About 45 rarely used classes and parts of the library as files of their
  own in LIBS:OpenMUI, loaded the first time a program needs one.
- The OpenMUI preferences program (SYS:Prefs/OpenMUI).
- [PLUGINS] MUI classes: NList, NListtree, TextEditor, BetterString,
  TheBar, Busy, Listtree and others, with their preference pages.
- Spot's UP-MCC 1.0 (spotUP, MIT, https://github.com/spotUP/selecta):
  eleven flat-look classes (UP-Button, UP-Text, UP-Panel, UP-Tabs,
  UP-Balance, UP-Slider, UP-Progress, UP-Cycle, UP-Field, UP-Scroller,
  UP-List), their preferences class UP-MCC.mcp, up-mcc.library, the Inter
  font and the Dark and Light themes, as Spot released them.
- Translations, an SDK with examples, and the full source.

The library file keeps the name muimaster.library and the library version
stays [VERSION]: programs depend on them. OpenMUI's own drawers are
LIBS:OpenMUI (its classes) and ENVARC:OpenMUI / ENV:OpenMUI (its
settings). Earlier test builds used LIBS:Zune, ENVARC:Zune and ENV:Zune:
installing over one moves the settings and takes the old files away.

Each program gets the MUI it asks for
-------------------------------------
A program written for MUI 3 sees MUI 3.8 (19.35), one asking for MUI 4
sees MUI 4, one asking for MUI 5 sees MUI 5 (21.227), and OpenMUI behaves
accordingly. In OpenMUI prefs, System page, "MUI version": "Behave as"
Automatic (the default), MUI 3.8, MUI 4 or MUI 5 for all programs, and a
list of programs with a MUI of their own.

Installing
----------
Double-click Install (Installer 43 or newer: on OS 3.1 use Installer
43.3 from Aminet, OS 3.2 has its own). The installer:
- looks for a MUI you already have (MUI 3.x, 4 or 5, its MUI: assign,
  its S:User-Startup lines, every muimaster.library in LIBS:) and tells
  you what it found. None of it is changed: OpenMUI goes into your first
  LIBS: drawer (normally SYS:Libs), where programs find it before MUI,
  and nothing is added to S:Startup-Sequence or S:User-Startup;
- keeps every file it replaces, and writes down every file it installs,
  in SYS:OpenMUI/Data;
- installs a bundled MUI class only when you do not have the same or a
  newer one yourself. The classes that come with a real MUI stay as they
  are for that MUI; OpenMUI uses its own;
- keeps your settings (ENVARC:OpenMUI) on an update (an earlier test
  build's in ENVARC:Zune are moved there), and does not touch MUI's
  (ENVARC:MUI);
- removes an earlier test build installed as Zune68k lean (the drawer
  SYS:Zune68k and SYS:Prefs/Zune) first, and puts your own files back.
Novice, Intermediate and Expert levels and pretend mode work.

Switching and removing
----------------------
SYS:OpenMUI has "Use real MUI" and "Use OpenMUI" (when a real MUI was
found) and "Uninstall". "Use real MUI" puts OpenMUI's libraries and
classes aside and puts your own files back: after a reboot programs get
your MUI. "Use OpenMUI" switches back. "Uninstall" (or Install again,
"Remove OpenMUI") deletes OpenMUI's files and puts back every file of
yours it had replaced; your MUI keeps working. The same from a Shell:
SYS:OpenMUI/Data/OpenMUISwitch MUI|OPENMUI|UNINSTALL|STATUS.

Status
------
A public beta. An earlier build is in daily use on an A1200 (PiStorm,
Emu68); the figures below come from emulators (FS-UAE, Amiberry). 184
real MUI programs and class demos (YAM, AmIRC, AmFTP, IBrowse, NList,
TextEditor, TheBar and many more) are tested against real
MUI 3.8 and MUI 5. MUI-Demo on OS 3.1 with 4 MB fast RAM: 460 KB for the
first program (MUI 3.8: 460 KB), 205 KB for each further one (MUI 3.8:
211-219 KB, MUI 5: 427-435 KB). Speed (cycle-exact emulation): at least
as fast as MUI 3.8 in 24 of 26 everyday operations, faster than MUI 5 in
all 27. Please report what works and what does not:
https://github.com/creep-ltx/openmui/issues

